WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:15.458
My name is Fillippo Bogliano, and I live in northern Italy.

2
00:00:15.459 --> 00:00:21.798
My home is on the southern slopes of the Alps in a city called Turin, which is also on the Po River.

3
00:00:21.799 --> 00:00:31.226
Many people travel here to trade with the local merchants for all kinds of products; some buy red wine while others come for our nutty chocolate.

4
00:00:31.227 --> 00:00:35.568
But Turin is best known for its cheese! How do I know this?

5
00:00:35.569 --> 00:00:40.496
Because I am a casaro, an expert cheese maker.

6
00:00:40.497 --> 00:00:46.942
My family first came to this area from France in 1745, when I was just a year old.

7
00:00:46.943 --> 00:00:54.696
Then my father herded cattle in the mountains and sold fresh milk, but this was hard work and did not pay very well.

8
00:00:54.697 --> 00:01:00.120
My father soon discovered that the casaros who bought his milk made a very nice living.

9
00:01:00.121 --> 00:01:05.001
So he decided that when I was old enough, I would learn the cheese-making trade.

10
00:01:05.002 --> 00:01:11.531
When I reached the age of 11, my father sent me to live with one of the most respected casaros in Turin.

11
00:01:11.532 --> 00:01:17.566
This man was glad to be my mentor since my father had taught me so much about milk already.

12
00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:23.840
I was an apprentice for five years, and I learned everything there was to know about making cheese.

13
00:01:23.841 --> 00:01:31.945
I quickly realized that no two batches of cheese are alike, just as no pail of fresh milk is exactly the same as another.

14
00:01:31.946 --> 00:01:39.217
I also learned that even a small change in the cheese-making process can result in a completely different kind of cheese.

15
00:01:39.218 --> 00:01:44.983
Now I am 22, and I have my own business, which keeps my whole family busy.

16
00:01:44.984 --> 00:01:53.249
My mother and father sell our cheese in the marketplace; my two brothers tend the cows, and my wife, Magda, manages the household.

17
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:57.247
We all work together because making cheese is difficult work.

18
00:01:57.248 --> 00:02:02.961
Several kinds of cheese are made in this region of Italy, but Gorgonzola is my favorite,

19
00:02:02.962 --> 00:02:08.405
because this white, creamy cheese with blue veins has a sharp, tangy flavor.

20
00:02:08.406 --> 00:02:12.966
It is the most famous cheese in Turin and my specialty.

21
00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:20.854
Legend has it that Gorgonzola was discovered by accident by an apprentice casaro who was very much in love.

22
00:02:20.855 --> 00:02:25.470
He was late for a meeting with his girlfriend, but he had not finished his work.

23
00:02:25.471 --> 00:02:31.029
So he dumped his unfinished cheese into a pail of fresh milk that a herdsman had brought in.

24
00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:39.410
Later the mixture hardened, became moldy, and turned greenish blue, but the casaro didn't want to throw it away.

25
00:02:39.411 --> 00:02:46.283
So he sold it anyway, and his customers loved it! Gorgonzola cheese was born.

26
00:02:46.284 --> 00:02:51.280
To be honest, though, making this wonderful cheese takes talent and patience.

27
00:02:51.281 --> 00:02:59.437
Every evening, after our herd comes down from the alpine meadows, my brothers milk the cows and bring the milk into my workshop.

28
00:02:59.438 --> 00:03:04.664
I carefully pour the milk into shallow pails and allow it to set overnight.

29
00:03:04.665 --> 00:03:08.873
The next day I add a special ingredient called rennet.

30
00:03:08.874 --> 00:03:14.570
Rennet is an acidic enzyme, a natural chemical produced in a calf's stomach.

31
00:03:14.571 --> 00:03:22.653
It turns the milk into a curd—a thickened milk paste—and separates the curd from the whey, a thin and yellowish liquid.

32
00:03:22.654 --> 00:03:31.125
In fact, this action is called "turning" the milk. On the morning of the second day, I make another curd from the morning milking.

33
00:03:31.126 --> 00:03:36.819
Then, in the afternoon, I combine the curd from the first day with the curd from the second day.

34
00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:42.659
I put the fresh curd on the bottom and sides of the pail and the older curd in the center.

35
00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:49.475
Next I gently knead the curd with my hands, smoothing it out, and remove it from the pail.

36
00:03:49.476 --> 00:03:53.822
Then I shape the cheese into a circular shape, called a round.

37
00:03:53.823 --> 00:04:03.702
Many types of cheese are made in this way, and cheese can be made in rounds of any size—some are small and some are quite large.

38
00:04:03.703 --> 00:04:12.241
I make about three rounds a day, and flip them over frequently to drain the remaining whey out of the curd.

39
00:04:12.242 --> 00:04:19.689
Most days there is no time to go back to the house for breakfast or lunch, so Magda brings pasta to my workshop.

40
00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:24.880
She doesn't like to come here very much because she thinks that the cheese is too stinky.

41
00:04:24.881 --> 00:04:28.733
She says that Gorgonzola smells like dirty socks!

42
00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:31.777
She is right; it does smell pretty bad.

43
00:04:31.778 --> 00:04:35.556
But I am so used to it now that it doesn't bother me.

44
00:04:35.557 --> 00:04:40.382
I just think about how good it will taste.

45
00:04:40.383 --> 00:04:47.274
The final step is to add sea salt to the rounds of curd over a period of five days to firm them up.

46
00:04:47.275 --> 00:04:53.474
Then the rounds are ready to be ripened, and my brothers and I take all the rounds to a nearby cave.

47
00:04:53.475 --> 00:05:00.253
We stack them on tall wooden racks like books on a library shelf, and as they go through the aging process,

48
00:05:00.254 --> 00:05:04.034
I return to the cave often to flip the rounds over.

49
00:05:04.035 --> 00:05:07.782
This keeps the rounds firm and hard as they age.

50
00:05:07.783 --> 00:05:13.882
Our caves in northern Italy have the perfect temperature and humidity for ripening the cheese.

51
00:05:13.883 --> 00:05:18.222
These special caves also have a mold that grows in them.

52
00:05:18.223 --> 00:05:28.071
It attaches itself to the ripening cheese and, like magic, causes the blue and green veins to appear in the cheese in about three weeks!

53
00:05:28.072 --> 00:05:33.703
We have to let the Gorgonzola ripen for at least three months before it is ready to eat.

54
00:05:33.704 --> 00:05:41.843
Younger cheese tastes sweeter and is softer. Rounds that are aged more than six months are harder and have a spicy flavor.

55
00:05:41.844 --> 00:05:48.320
Older Gorgonzola that is aged for a year is more valuable, so we mostly make this kind.

56
00:05:48.321 --> 00:05:56.271
When the cheese rounds are ready, my brothers carry them to the wagon so that my mother and father can take them to the market the next day.

57
00:05:56.272 --> 00:06:05.583
At our stall, Bogliano's Gorgonzola, everyone can stop and taste our cheese, drink a little wine, and talk about the lovely weather.

58
00:06:05.584 --> 00:06:14.649
Oh, I wish I could eat and talk with our customers, but I must stay behind to clean up the caves and watch over the next batch of cheese as it ages.

59
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:20.695
My cheese-making work never ends, but I love it!
